 uage learning\, schools\, or other topics? A Settlement Worker can help. D
 rop in to meet our settlement worker every Tuesday afternoon from 2 pm-5 p
 m.\nVisit our branch or contact Ada Tao at 416-770-0312 or atao@cathcrossc
 ultural.org.\nIn partnership with Catholic Crosscultural Services.\nPlease
  bring your immigration documents (PR card) with you
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Front Entrance
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c1d0efbfe6da1042250aff
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Housing Help
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T170000
UID:69c1d0455a2d780f42c48ab3@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Drop in and work one-on-one with staff from The Housing Help C
 entre (THHC) to determine your housing needs\, connect you to other govern
 ment services\, and to match you with landlords across the city. THHC work
 s with both individuals and families in the City of Toronto to find and ma
 intain affordable\, safe housing.\nThe Housing Help Centre can be contacte
 d at 416-285-8070 or at shhc.ca.
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Front Entrance
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c1d0455a2d780f42c48ab3
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tea & Movies: Gilda (1946)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T160000
UID:69b1e74b4dacf581ff8146ac@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an afternoon of classic movies. Tea & coffee will 
 be served.\nGilda (1946)\nA small-time gambler hired to work in a Buenos A
 ires casino discovers his employer's new wife is his former lover.\nNo reg
 istration required.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69b1e74b4dacf581ff8146ac
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Computers for Beginners Series
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260821T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260821T120000
UID:6a32f17d4407827f8aa59b2b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Computers for Beginners is a series of 6 classes which have be
 en designed to take a learner with no\, or very few skills\, from zero to 
 a solid grounding in the absolute basics. Each class is set up to connect 
 with the previous and next class in a series format. Registration is requi
 red for the series.\nFriday July 10: Use the Mouse\nFriday July 17: Use th
 e Keyboard\nFriday July 31: Use the Web\nFriday August 7: Use Google Searc
 h and Maps\nFriday August 14: Use Windows\nFriday August 21: Use Email –
  Gmail\nCourse Objective\nUpon completion of this class\, students will be
  able to use a computer mouse and keyboards to navigate the Internet\, Win
 dows\, conduct Google Searches\, and use Gmail.\nPrerequisites: None\nForm
 at: Hands-on with demonstrations
LOCATION:Humberwood
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a32f17d4407827f8aa59b2b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mending & Embroidery
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260808T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260808T150000
UID:6a5f8e6fc7e02e3d006aa1d3@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Bring some well-loved clothing items for a mend or refresh! Le
 arn or practice how to mend wear and tear\, or add some embroidered embell
 ishments to showcase your own style.\nThis program provides space\, help\,
  and materials for learning mending and embroidery techniques.  Participan
 ts will be introduced to a craft that promotes sustainability through a fu
 n\, affordable\, and beautiful way to mend and embellish clothing or other
  textiles. Drop-in program\, no registration required. Note that this is 
 not a class but we can provide some resources and supplies for learning a
 nd practicing.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information o
 n this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Li
 braries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will onl
 y be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Qu
 estions about how the library handles your information can be directed to 
 Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street
 \, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Cedarbrae\, First Floor Main Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5f8e6fc7e02e3d006aa1d3
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Group Art Therapy with the Alzheimer Society of Toronto
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260817T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260817T153000
UID:6a594f8388e9bf2800315fee@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join this four-part Group Art Therapy series! Through individu
 al and collaborative creative activities\, participants will have the oppo
 rtunity to explore and express thoughts\, feelings\, and experiences in a 
 supportive and welcoming environment. Using a variety of artistic material
 s and mediums\, the group will discover new ways to connect\, reflect\, an
 d communicate through the creative process. No previous art experience is 
 required.\nThis program series is presented in collaboration with the Alzh
 eimer Society of Toronto and is designed for people living with early to m
 id stage dementia or mild cognitive impairment\, or together with a care p
 artner.\nAbout the facilitator: Kathleen Downie is an arts educator who ha
 s led many engaging art programs with the Alzheimer Society of Toronto ove
 r the years. Kathleen is currently completing her Art Therapy training thr
 ough the Vancouver Art Therapy Institute (VATI) Distance Program. As part 
 of her training\, Kathleen will facilitate group art therapy sessions for 
 individuals living with mild to moderate dementia or mild cognitive impair
 ment (MCI)\, along with their care partners.\nSnacks are provided for all 
 participants. Ingredient lists will be available at the program.\nThe Alzh
 eimer Society of Toronto is solely responsible for the protection of perso
 nal information collected through the program registration process. Regist
 ration closes after week 1\, or when full.\nLINK\nhttps://alz.to/calendar/
 group-art-therapy-with-kathleen/2026-08-10/\nThis program is a Collaborati
 ve program with the Alzheimer Society of Toronto and Toronto Public Librar
 y.
LOCATION:Eglinton Square\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a594f8388e9bf2800315fee
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
