END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Alderwood Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T200000
UID:6a46ba046db61d2800998518@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:July's title is "The Future" by Catherine Leroux. "The Future"
  was the 2024 winner of Canada Reads\, and is set in an alternate\, dystop
 ian history where Detroit was never surrendered to the U.S. A grandmother
  searches for her missing granddaughters and discovers a resilient communi
 ty and a hidden society of feral children fighting to survive.
LOCATION:Alderwood
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a46ba046db61d2800998518
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:What is Artificial Intelligence?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260817T133000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260817T143000
UID:6a4d4d009302a0b2287b1c70@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:AI technologies have become increasingly prevalent in many asp
 ects of modern life\, including healthcare\, finance\, transportation\, an
 d entertainment. Some examples of AI applications include virtual assistan
 ts like Siri and Alexa\, self-driving cars\, fraud detection systems\, and
  personalized recommendations in online shopping. Come join this beginner 
 in-person class to learn what AI is\, how it learns and predicts outcomes\
 , considerations when using GenAI applications\, and how the Internet of T
 hings (IOT) applies to AI.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal i
 nformation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under t
 he Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informat
 ion will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and ev
 aluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be 
 directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 
 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Woodside Square\, Program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a4d4d009302a0b2287b1c70
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Resilience Reads: a climate neighbourhood reading party
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T200000
UID:69dd3dd5c1cf6f9a758518d1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us to share the joy of reading\, while building climate r
 esilience knowledge. Members are invited to discuss a book they are readin
 g\, tell stories\,  and empower each other to explore community-driven sol
 utions. Each party will start with a quiet read and reflect time\, with op
 portunities to join group discussions later on over refreshments.\nAPRIL 1
 5-Learning from trees for Earth Month\nThe Overstory\nby Richard Power\nMA
 Y 20-All women are water defenders\nMemory of Water\nby Emmi Itaranta or y
 our favourite book featuring water!\nJUNE 17– Righting our relationship 
 with land\nEarth Keeper\nby N. Scott Momaday or your favourite book that s
 hares how words are medicine!\nJULY 15– Climate migration or the borders
  between us are not lines that divide us\, but seams that connect us\nGun 
 Island\n/\nThe Hungry Tide\nby Amitav Ghosh or your favourite island setti
 ng summer read!\nCopies are available in the branch to pick up\, while sup
 plies last.\nAUGUST 19--Fossil Fuel Folly\nAmerican War\nby Omar El Akkad
  or your favourite dystopian read!\nSEPTEMBER 16--We Need Pollinators\nTh
 e History of Bees\nby Maja Lunde or your favourite animal-centred book!\n
 OCTOBER 21--Climate Change is Scary\nThe Annual Migration of Clouds\nby Pr
 emee Mohamed or a different book that shares a scary future ecological cri
 sis!\nNOVEMBER 18--Climate changes everything\nThe Ministry for the Future
 \nby Kim Stanley Robinson or your favourite time travel read!\nInformatio
 n Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected b
 y the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(
 a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pr
 ogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the libr
 ary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswe
 rline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Parkdale\, Auditorium A
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69dd3dd5c1cf6f9a758518d1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
